# Practical: Set Up and Manage Version Control with Git and GitHub

### Aim
To set up Git and GitHub and perform basic version-control operations such as **initialize, add, commit, branch, merge, and push/pull**.

---

## Step 1 — Install Git

Download Git from the official website:

[Git official website](https://git-scm.com/downloads?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Install it using the default options.

After installation, open **Command Prompt** or **PowerShell** and check:

```bash
git --version
```

You should get something like:

```text
git version 2.x.x
```

If you see the version, Git is installed correctly. ✅

---

# Step 2 — Create a GitHub account

Go to:

[GitHub](https://github.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Create an account or log in.

You will need your:

- GitHub username
- GitHub email
- GitHub password

**Important:** Your GitHub password is not normally used directly by Git for command-line authentication. GitHub commonly uses browser authentication, a personal access token, or SSH.

---

# Step 3 — Configure Git

Open Command Prompt/PowerShell.

Set your name:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
```

Example:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Arun Kumar"
```

Set your email:

```bash
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

Example:

```bash
git config --global user.email "arun@gmail.com"
```

Check the configuration:

```bash
git config --list
```

You should see:

```text
user.name=Arun Kumar
user.email=arun@gmail.com
```

---

# Step 4 — Create a project

Let's create a simple project called **git-demo**.

On Windows:

```bash
mkdir git-demo
cd git-demo
```

Check your current folder:

```bash
cd
```

---

# Step 5 — Create a file

Create a file called:

```text
index.html
```

Put this inside:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Git Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello Git and GitHub!</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

Save the file.

Your project now looks like:

```text
git-demo
└── index.html
```

---

# Step 6 — Initialize Git

Inside the `git-demo` folder, execute:

```bash
git init
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
Initialized empty Git repository in .../git-demo/.git/
```

🎉 Your project is now a **Git repository**.

---

# Step 7 — Check the repository status

Run:

```bash
git status
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
Untracked files:
    index.html
```

**Untracked** means Git sees the file but isn't tracking it yet.

---

# Step 8 — Add the file to the staging area

Run:

```bash
git add index.html
```

Or add all files:

```bash
git add .
```

Now check:

```bash
git status
```

You should see:

```text
Changes to be committed:
    new file: index.html
```

---

# Step 9 — Commit the file

A **commit** saves a version of your project.

Run:

```bash
git commit -m "Initial commit"
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
[master/main abc1234] Initial commit
 1 file changed
```

Your first version is now saved in Git. ✅

---

# Step 10 — Create a GitHub repository

Go to:

[GitHub](https://github.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Click:

**+ → New repository**

Enter:

```text
Repository name: git-demo
```

You can choose **Public** for a normal college practical.

### Important

When creating the repository, for this exercise, **do not initialize it with**:

- README
- `.gitignore`
- License

We'll connect our existing local repository.

Click:

**Create repository**

---

# Step 11 — Connect your local Git repository to GitHub

GitHub will show commands for your new repository.

You can use:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/git-demo.git
```

Replace `YOUR_USERNAME` with your GitHub username.

For example:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/arunkumar/git-demo.git
```

Check the remote:

```bash
git remote -v
```

You should see your GitHub repository URL.

---

# Step 12 — Rename the branch to `main`

Run:

```bash
git branch -M main
```

Now your current branch is called `main`.

Check:

```bash
git branch
```

You should see:

```text
* main
```

---

# Step 13 — Push your project to GitHub

Run:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

Git will authenticate you with GitHub if necessary.

After a successful push, you should see something like:

```text
Branch 'main' set up to track remote branch 'main' from 'origin'.
```

🎉 Your project is now on GitHub.

Open your repository in your browser and you should see:

```text
git-demo
│
└── index.html
```

---

# Step 14 — Make another change

Open `index.html`.

Change:

```html
<h1>Hello Git and GitHub!</h1>
```

to:

```html
<h1>Welcome to Git and GitHub!</h1>
```

Save it.

Check the status:

```bash
git status
```

Git will tell you that `index.html` has been modified.

---

# Step 15 — See what changed

Run:

```bash
git diff
```

This shows the changes you made.

---

# Step 16 — Add and commit the change

Run:

```bash
git add index.html
```

Then:

```bash
git commit -m "Update welcome message"
```

Now push the new version:

```bash
git push
```

Refresh your GitHub repository.

The updated file should be there. ✅

---

# Step 17 — View commit history

Run:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

You might see:

```text
7a21abc Update welcome message
2b45def Initial commit
```

This shows the different versions of your project.

---

# Step 18 — Create a branch

Branches allow you to work on a new feature without immediately changing the main version.

Create a branch:

```bash
git branch feature
```

Switch to it:

```bash
git switch feature
```

Or use the shorter command:

```bash
git switch -c feature
```

Check:

```bash
git branch
```

You should see:

```text
* feature
  main
```

The `*` indicates your current branch.

---

# Step 19 — Make a change in the branch

Modify `index.html`.

For example, add:

```html
<p>This is my feature branch.</p>
```

Then:

```bash
git add .
```

Commit it:

```bash
git commit -m "Add feature paragraph"
```

---

# Step 20 — Merge the branch

First return to `main`:

```bash
git switch main
```

Then merge your feature:

```bash
git merge feature
```

Your feature is now included in `main`.

---

# Step 21 — Push the merged version

Run:

```bash
git push origin main
```

Your GitHub repository now contains the merged changes.

---

# Step 22 — Clone a GitHub repository

This is useful when you want to download an existing project.

Go to your GitHub repository and copy its HTTPS URL.

Then run:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/git-demo.git
```

Git downloads the repository to your computer.

Move into it:

```bash
cd git-demo
```

Check:

```bash
git status
```

---

# Step 23 — Pull changes from GitHub

If somebody has changed the GitHub repository, you can download the latest version using:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

So:

**Push = computer → GitHub**

```text
git push
```

**Pull = GitHub → computer**

```text
git pull
```

---

# ⭐ Commands you should remember for your practical

```bash
git --version
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"

git init
git status
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"

git remote add origin <repository-url>
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

git pull
git clone <repository-url>

git branch
git switch -c feature
git switch main
git merge feature

git log --oneline
git diff
```

## The complete workflow

Remember this sequence:

```text
             LOCAL COMPUTER
                   │
              git init
                   ↓
              git add .
                   ↓
          git commit -m "..."
                   │
                   ↓
                git push
                   │
                   ▼
              ┌─────────┐
              │ GitHub  │
              └─────────┘
                   │
                git pull
                   ↓
             LOCAL COMPUTER
```

